 abstract=f'''Predicting spatial stress concentrations from measured weld geometry requires a representation that remains usable when only part of the surface is observed. We investigated this problem using thirteen processed weld-front geometries from a public fatigue research repository, grouped by five documentary parent plates. Targets were regularized tensile maximum-principal-stress fields from previously qualified quadratic-tetrahedral finite element models with a standardized planar rear and nominal100MPa elastic loading. These are numerical reference fields, not experimental stress measurements. Frozen neural models were subjected to an eight-cell factorial intervention on coordinate centering, geometry spans and surface-normal statistics. One nineteen-channel representation derived from visible local geometry and a registered query-plan datum was then evaluated using ten fixed Point Transformer fits and five fixed XGBoost fits. All predictions retained the original source coordinates, area weights and complete34200-point core. For full observation, two-seed equal-plate normalized L2 was{n1['FULL'].L2:.4f} for the original neural model,{n2['FULL'].L2:.4f} for context-mixed training and{n3['FULL'].L2:.4f} for the new representation; XGBoost achieved{b2['FULL'].L2:.4f}. Under the narrowest observation, the new representation achieved L2{n3['X43'].L2:.4f} and High95 area IoU{n3['X43'].high95_IoU:.4f}. The prescribed joint accuracy, localization and amplitude safeguards classified the constructive result as{decision.replace('_',' ').lower()}. Controlled input substitutions demonstrate computational representation sensitivity, while local observability alone does not establish predictive superiority. Repeated development on these small, previously examined groups limits independent generalization claims. Physical stress accuracy, unregistered-scan transfer and fatigue-life prediction remain unvalidated.'''
